steps.body:
The pipeline's `Backtest` gives you one night's P&L. A desk judges a strategy over many nights, and every number it reads starts from the **equity curve**: the capital after each day, which is the starting capital plus the P&L of every day so far. That's money, so it stays in integer ticks.

A **return** is a day's P&L as a fraction of the capital that earned it, so strategies of any size compare. There are two kinds, and both are doubles, since a ratio isn't money:

- The **simple return**, `(V1 - V0) / V0`, from the day's opening equity `V0` to its closing equity `V1`. The simple returns of your positions, weighted by size, add up to the portfolio's, so risk reports use them.
- The **log return**, `std::log(V1 / V0)`. Log returns add up over time: their sum over a year is the log of the year's growth.

Simple returns don't add up over time, and that's how they mislead. Up 50% and then down 50% averages 0%, but compounding multiplies:

```cpp
const double up = 0.50;
const double down = -0.50;
std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
std::cout << "average " << (up + down) / 2 << '\n';                                     // average 0.0000
std::cout << "growth  " << (1 + up) * (1 + down) - 1 << '\n';                           // growth  -0.2500
std::cout << "logs    " << std::log(1 + up) + std::log(1 + down) << '\n';               // logs    -0.2877
std::cout << "exp - 1 " << std::exp(std::log(1 + up) + std::log(1 + down)) - 1 << '\n';  // exp - 1 -0.2500
```

Averaging daily returns overstates the rate a strategy grows at, and the more it swings, the more it overstates.

**Maximum drawdown** is the deepest fall of the equity curve below its *peak*, the highest it has been so far. It measures the pain of holding on: a desk cuts a trader's limits after a set drawdown, and a fund's investors leave after one. Keep the running peak and the worst gap below it, in O(1) a day. It misleads because it comes from one path. The same days in another order fall further, though their average and spread don't change:

```cpp
// The deepest fall below the running peak, in ticks, starting from 0.
int64_t max_drawdown(const std::vector<int64_t>& pnl) {
    int64_t equity = 0, peak = 0, worst = 0;
    for (int64_t p : pnl) {
        equity += p;
        peak = std::max(peak, equity);
        worst = std::max(worst, peak - equity);
    }
    return worst;
}

std::vector<int64_t> days{300, -200, 300, -200, 300, -200};
std::cout << max_drawdown(days) << '\n';  // 200
std::sort(days.begin(), days.end());      // the same six days, losses first
std::cout << max_drawdown(days) << '\n';  // 600
```

A longer history only digs deeper, too, so a three-month backtest's drawdown is no bound on next year's.

