    std::cout << "slippage  " << std::setw(7) << -s.slippage / 100.0 << '\n';
    std::cout << "fees      " << std::setw(7) << -s.fees / 100.0 << '\n';
    std::cout << "net       " << std::setw(7) << net / 100.0 << '\n';
    std::cout << "per share: edge " << double(s.gross()) / s.traded << " ticks, costs "
              << double(s.slippage + s.fees) / s.traded << " ticks\n";
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A backtest that looks too good usually is. Three biases cause most of it.

**Look-ahead bias** is trading on data you couldn't have had yet. Usually it's one index: the position held over day `d` must be decided by the close of day `d - 1`, and a signal that reads `closes[d]` has seen the move it's paid for. Other leaks work alike: a z-score against the whole day's mean, which includes ticks still to come, or an earnings figure dated to its quarter instead of the day it was published.

**Survivorship bias** is testing only on the companies still around. Today's index members are the ones that survived, so a backtest on them never holds a stock that went bankrupt:

```cpp
// A year's returns for the 8 stocks in an index on January 1. Two went bankrupt.
const std::vector<double> returns{0.12, 0.08, -0.05, 0.21, 0.03, -1.00, 0.15, -1.00};
double all = 0.0, survivors = 0.0;
int alive = 0;
for (double r : returns) {
    all += r;
    if (r > -1.0) {  // still listed on December 31
        survivors += r;
        ++alive;
    }
}
std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
std::cout << "survivors " << survivors / alive << '\n';     // survivors 0.0900
std::cout << "everyone  " << all / returns.size() << '\n';  // everyone  -0.1825
```

The fix is data that keeps the *delisted* names, the ones the exchange dropped, with their last prices.

**Overfitting** is fitting the noise. Try enough rules and one fits the past by luck, since a year pins a Sharpe to only about ±1. The guard is an **out-of-sample** test: choose the rule and its parameters on one stretch of data, the *in-sample* period, then judge it once on a later stretch you never looked at. Tune on that stretch and it's in-sample too. Count the rules you tried as well: on prices where nothing can earn, the best of 40 rules over six months averages a Sharpe between 2 and 3.

The example backtests momentum on daily closes. It holds 100 shares **long**, owned so that a rise pays, when the price rose over the last 10 days, its *lookback*. It holds 100 **short**, sold from borrowed shares so that a fall pays, when the price fell. The closes are *random walks*, moving a random -10 to 10 ticks a day. No rule can earn on one, which makes it a test: an honest backtest's average Sharpe there must be near 0.

example.code:
#include <cmath>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

struct Stats {  // Welford's running mean and variance
    long n = 0;
    double mean = 0.0;
    double m2 = 0.0;
    void add(double x) {
        ++n;
        const double d = x - mean;
        mean += d / n;
        m2 += d * (x - mean);
    }
    double stdev() const { return n > 1 ? std::sqrt(m2 / (n - 1)) : 0.0; }
    double sharpe() const { return stdev() > 0 ? mean / stdev() * std::sqrt(252.0) : 0.0; }
};

// A year of daily closes in ticks: a random walk that moves -10 to 10 ticks a day.
std::vector<int64_t> random_closes(std::uint64_t seed, int days) {
    std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
    std::vector<int64_t> closes{10000};
    for (int d = 0; d < days; ++d) {
        closes.push_back(closes.back() + static_cast<int64_t>(rng() % 21) - 10);
    }
    return closes;
}

// Momentum: hold 100 shares long over day d if the price rose over the lookback, short if it fell.
Stats backtest(const std::vector<int64_t>& closes, int lookback, bool peek) {
    Stats daily;
    for (std::size_t d = lookback + 1; d < closes.size(); ++d) {
        const std::size_t last = peek ? d : d - 1;  // the bug: d is a close you don't know yet
        const int64_t change = closes[last] - closes[last - lookback];
        const int64_t position = change > 0 ? 100 : (change < 0 ? -100 : 0);
        daily.add(double(position * (closes[d] - closes[d - 1])));  // the day's P&L in ticks
    }
    return daily;
}

int main() {
    std::uint64_t first_seed = 0;
    int markets = 0;
    std::cin >> first_seed >> markets;
    Stats honest_all, peek_all;
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    for (int m = 0; m < markets; ++m) {
        const std::vector<int64_t> closes = random_closes(first_seed + m, 252);
        const Stats honest = backtest(closes, 10, false);
        const Stats peek = backtest(closes, 10, true);
        honest_all.add(honest.sharpe());
        peek_all.add(peek.sharpe());
        std::cout << "market " << first_seed + m << ": honest " << honest.sharpe() << ", peeking " << peek.sharpe() << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "average: honest " << honest_all.mean << ", peeking " << peek_all.mean << '\n';
    return 0;
}

